WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.290
- What is more serious,

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.062
a head injury or a foot injury?

00:00:03.062 --> 00:00:03.916
- A head injury.

00:00:03.916 --> 00:00:04.749
(Dr. Mike laughs loudly)

00:00:04.749 --> 00:00:06.320
- The Patreon community requested it.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.190
You requested it.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:08.300
We're watching "The Office",

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.600
Checking out the medical scenes

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.910
watching them do some of these

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:11.860
new merchant alert!

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.230
You could buy it right there,

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.063
down below.

00:00:14.063 --> 00:00:16.670
Hundred percent of the
proceeds are going to charity.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.030
Click buy. Click and watch.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:19.010
Let's do it. Bewoop!

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.950
- Just say again, really loudly.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:21.783
What happened?

00:00:21.783 --> 00:00:22.710
- [Michael] Okay. (frustrated groans)

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.120
I burned my foot,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.450
very badly on my Foreman Grill

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.210
and I now need someone to
come and bring me into work.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:30.490
- How do you burn your foot

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:31.590
on a George Foreman Grill?

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:32.988
- I enjoy having breakfast in bed.

00:00:32.988 --> 00:00:35.130
I like waking up to the smell of bacon.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:35.963
Sue me.

00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:37.360
- I don't really even
recommend eating bacon.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.960
cause it's a processed meat,

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.530
which has a cancer risk.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:41.720
I mean, if you have it every now and then

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:42.640
it's not the end of the world.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.990
Do not cook it in bed

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.050
while you're asleep with a hot-- ugh

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.060
- Today, I got up,

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.140
I stepped onto the grill

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.380
and it clamped down on my foot.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.400
- Oh! That looks so fake, by the way.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.530
It would be burned.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:54.560
It would be bloody.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:55.940
There would be skin peeling.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.030
That looks like he just sc--

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.270
like a little cat scratch.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.400
I had the worst experience
when I was a kid.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.630
My sister was using one of these pots.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.900
The pot actually you use to steam

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.080
and like breathe over
to help your sinuses.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.330
She asked me to bring it from
the kitchen after it boiled.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.300
And as I'm bringing it,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.930
I spill it all over my crotch.
(woman screaming)

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.447
Oh my god!

00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:15.455
- (crash)

00:01:15.455 --> 00:01:16.288
- What was that?

00:01:16.288 --> 00:01:17.381
- [Pam] What was that?

00:01:17.381 --> 00:01:18.740
- [Jim] Oh!

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:19.953
He hit the pole.

00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:22.146
(beeping)

00:01:22.146 --> 00:01:23.451
It's broken, right? He can't--

00:01:23.451 --> 00:01:24.284
- [Pam] Oh my gosh.

00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:25.760
[Jim] Oh Dwight. Dwight.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.380
- [Everyone] Oh!
(exclaims in disgust)

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:29.430
- If you're throwing up

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.500
immediately after a car accident,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.790
that's a potential sign
for serious head trauma.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.070
You better be calling 911.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.400
- I am feeling better.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.810
My body's literally healing itself.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.917
It is amazing what the
human body is capable of

00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:43.760
when you have a powerful brain.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.750
- [Ryan] I ground up four
extra strength aspirin

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.790
and put them in Michael's pudding.

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.340
I do the same thing with my dog

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.030
to get him to take his heartworm medicine.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.970
- Don't give people medicines

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.400
that they're not ready for.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.810
Aspirin, especially.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.640
It has all sorts of interactions
with other medications.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.530
Some people are sensitive to aspirin.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.860
Like if I take an aspirin
tablet by nose bleeds

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.680
cause it actually impairs
platelet function.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.740
If God forbid they have
a procedure coming up

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.360
that would be detrimental.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.850
Children, if you give them aspirin,

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.520
they can develop Reye's Syndrome.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.760
Please, don't just dose
people with aspirin,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.050
just cause you do the
same thing with your dog.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.859
Bear's good at taking pills.
(barks)

00:02:18.859 --> 00:02:19.944
(soft thud)

00:02:19.944 --> 00:02:20.777
- (faintly groans)

00:02:20.777 --> 00:02:23.560
- Oh. Okay, I think we need
to take him to the hospital.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:24.810
- [Dwight] No.
- [Michael] Oh no.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.570
- I'm pretty sure he has a concussion.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:28.940
- Oh, now you feel some
compassion for him.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.130
- He needs to go right now

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:31.470
and you're his emergency contact.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.527
I think you should go with him.

00:02:32.527 --> 00:02:33.520
- Why don't you go with him?

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.770
- I barely know him.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:35.613
- I want Michael to take me.

00:02:35.613 --> 00:02:37.720
- I can't take you. I don't have my car.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.330
- Why don't they just call 911?

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.260
- [Jim] Wake up.
- [Dwight] Ahh!

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.093
- Oh no, don't do that.

00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:41.926
- [Pam] Let me help you Dwight, c'mon.

00:02:41.926 --> 00:02:42.759
- Okay Pam. Thanks.

00:02:42.759 --> 00:02:43.858
- Get up. Get up.

00:02:43.858 --> 00:02:44.691
- You're the best.

00:02:44.691 --> 00:02:45.524
- [Jim] Just keep him awake.

00:02:45.524 --> 00:02:46.957
- Smells like chicken soup.

00:02:46.957 --> 00:02:47.790
- I know.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:48.623
- When you have a concussion,

00:02:48.623 --> 00:02:49.950
you need cognitive rest.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.320
Less lights. Less sounds.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.340
Less interaction. Less movement.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.030
You need your brain to recover.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.382
And the worst concussion is
actually your second concussion.

00:02:57.382 --> 00:02:59.340
God forbid, you ever get a concussion

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.450
in let's say, a game.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.640
Do not go back into the game

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.330
cause the second concussion's
gonna be much worse.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.310
- What is more serious, a head injury

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.265
or a foot injury?

00:03:06.265 --> 00:03:07.098
- A head injury.

00:03:07.098 --> 00:03:07.948
- [Michael] Well--

00:03:07.948 --> 00:03:08.781
(Dr. Mike laughs loudly)

00:03:08.781 --> 00:03:09.614
- So I'm ordering a CAT scan.

00:03:09.614 --> 00:03:11.010
- [Dwight] What is that?

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.275
- The reason we get CT scans

00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:15.000
when someone's involved in
a car accident, for example,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.950
is because if you have
bleeding in the brain

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.330
it's an enclosed environment.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.360
The skull actually prevents blood

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.380
from being able to displace the skin.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.900
And as a result, pressure builds up.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:24.927
And that could be really problematic.

00:03:24.927 --> 00:03:26.850
Outside of the fact
that you're not getting

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.050
enough circulation to the brain.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.160
We actually follow the
specific set of guidelines

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.100
known as the Canadian Head CT guidelines.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.360
You basically look

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:35.790
at what the patient experienced.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:37.330
Look at the type of trauma they had.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:38.760
What symptoms they're experiencing.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.990
You plug in the score

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:41.470
and it basically recommends whether or not

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.610
you should have a CT scan.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.550
Remember that most CT scans expose you

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.240
to as much radiation as between

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.500
a hundred and a thousand x-rays.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.280
So if you don't need that excess radiation

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.380
you really shouldn't be getting it.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:52.470
- I'm having contractions

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:53.610
but they're irregular

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:54.710
and far apart.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.510
So I'm not really in labor.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.060
I'm near labor.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.150
- Sometimes you can
have false contractions

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.030
when you get closer

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:02.810
called Braxton Hicks contractions.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:03.810
Sometimes they're not sure.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:04.643
That's why they call our

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:05.476
on-call answering service

00:04:05.476 --> 00:04:06.340
and we give them some guidance.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.830
- [Kelly] Labor can last weeks.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.090
And they take your insides out

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:10.580
and they just plop them on a table.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.410
And sometimes epidurals don't work.

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:13.710
You can poop yourself.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.670
- A lot of people poop during deliveries.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.220
I think I delivered
like 30, maybe 40 babies

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.710
during my residency training

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:20.543
and I would put the poop rate

00:04:20.543 --> 00:04:22.860
somewhere around 60, 70 percent.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.831
Safety of the baby and
safety of the mother.

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:27.350
Most important, most
important, most important.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:28.183
Not the poop.

00:04:28.183 --> 00:04:29.510
Never thought I'd say that statement.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:30.343
- [Michael] Oh hey hey.

00:04:30.343 --> 00:04:31.890
Um, should I bring a
dictionary to the hospital?

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.550
- The hospital will provide dictionaries,

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:34.485
bring a Thesaurus.

00:04:34.485 --> 00:04:36.420
(mumbles) need a dictionary or a Thesaurus

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.253
in a hospital.

00:04:37.253 --> 00:04:38.095
- This is ridiculous.

00:04:38.095 --> 00:04:38.940
- [Pam] Dwight get away.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.685
- No no no. Dwight, let
Jim do that, please?

00:04:40.685 --> 00:04:41.988
- I didn't know we had a tape measure.

00:04:41.988 --> 00:04:43.560
- We don't.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.820
- Was he going to measure her
cervix dilation with that?

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:48.380
We do not put tape measures

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.570
inside human bodies to measure anything.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.720
Again, another statement I
never thought I would make.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:53.710
- Pam's doing great.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.350
Uh, she's 10 inches dilated now.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:56.830
S-sorry, meters.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:57.663
Centimeters.

00:04:57.663 --> 00:04:59.130
- (laughs) 10 meters dilate--

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.040
She's 30 feet dilated!

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.470
- And she's also fully faced,

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:03.810
which I don't know what that is.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.349
- [Dr. Mike] It means the baby's a-coming.

00:05:05.349 --> 00:05:08.470
- It's only been six-- nineteen hours.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:09.910
- Halpert Room D1.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:12.230
Alright. Family only beyond this point.

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:13.063
Thank you.

00:05:13.063 --> 00:05:14.640
- It actually says
"Patients and staff only."

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.090
Normally we have a check-in desk

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.810
where uh, visitors have to check in

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.200
and then we check to see

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.750
if it's a good time for them to come in.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:22.040
We don't just let people barge it

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.116
in the midst of a delivery.

00:05:23.116 --> 00:05:25.065
- [Jim] Babe, she's so beautiful.

00:05:25.065 --> 00:05:26.220
(baby cries)

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.670
- Crying baby means a healthy baby.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:30.470
- Why is he smoking a cigar?

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:32.136
- Yeah.

00:05:32.136 --> 00:05:33.345
That's right.

00:05:33.345 --> 00:05:34.463
It's a baby, see.

00:05:34.463 --> 00:05:36.220
- Yeah you shouldn't smoke in a hospital

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:37.753
where there's oxygen tanks.

00:05:37.753 --> 00:05:38.586
- Ready? (baby cries)

00:05:38.586 --> 00:05:39.850
One, two, three.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:41.400
- That's called the burrito technique.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.210
Very important to learn early on

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.660
cause it helps coddle the
baby and helps him sleep.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.650
- Careful though. You
don't wanna break it--

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:48.864
- She's not gonna, just--

00:05:48.864 --> 00:05:49.970
(baby cries)

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:50.803
- I got it.

00:05:50.803 --> 00:05:52.360
- Just the other day I had a,

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:53.510
first time parents come in.

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:55.370
They're nervous and they're good nervous

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:56.203
because they're excited.

00:05:56.203 --> 00:05:57.550
They want to do the right thing.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:59.210
They're asking a bunch of questions.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.470
But it's awesome cause then the baby

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:02.390
comes in for sometimes
a newborn weight check,

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:03.570
or sometimes they come in right away

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.060
at the two month first
visit for vaccinations.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.330
Then four months. Then six months.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:08.630
And you get to see the baby grow like,

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.470
that's it just, it's one
of the most fun parts

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.303
about being a doctor.

00:06:11.303 --> 00:06:13.820
- I just, I can't tell if
she's getting anything.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:14.653
- Really?

00:06:14.653 --> 00:06:16.810
- Doesn't feel right.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:19.490
- Well you, you're pushing
the milk out, right?

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.130
- How would one do that?

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.959
- Wasn't it, it's kinda like a--

00:06:23.959 --> 00:06:25.480
- No, you don't push milk out.

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:26.710
The baby has to latch.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.130
And sometimes it's a process.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:29.301
The baby has a tongue tie.

00:06:29.301 --> 00:06:30.700
The breast anatomy.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.050
Sometimes it can be problematic,

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:33.130
but it's all salvable.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:35.600
We have lactation specialists
that meet with the moms.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.310
Perhaps they can do some supplementation,

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:38.430
especially early on.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.620
Really depends on each individual case.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:41.760
But the general recommendation

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.300
is to be breastfeeding your child.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:44.133
- [Dwight] Fire!

00:06:44.133 --> 00:06:44.966
- Not real.

00:06:44.966 --> 00:06:46.424
This was merely a training exercise.

00:06:46.424 --> 00:06:47.520
(loud crash)

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:48.353
- What?

00:06:48.353 --> 00:06:50.468
- [Dwight] So, what have we learned?

00:06:50.468 --> 00:06:51.301
(soft thud)

00:06:51.301 --> 00:06:53.380
- [Dwight] Oh come on.
It's not real Stanley.

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:54.660
Don't have a heart attack.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:55.560
- Check for a pulse.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:56.450
Call for help.

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:58.779
If he's not pulsating
and he's not breathing

00:06:58.779 --> 00:06:59.612
(squeakily says) chest compressions,

00:06:59.612 --> 00:07:01.560
chest compressions, chest compressions.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:02.393
- Andy

00:07:02.393 --> 00:07:05.491
- A throne for Your Highness.

00:07:05.491 --> 00:07:07.300
- I'm not sitting in a wheelchair.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:08.480
- No, no, no, no debate.

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:09.900
You are going to sit in that wheelchair.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.030
- You actually don't want to
be sitting in a wheelchair

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.140
when you don't want to.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.290
That could actually de-condition
your heart, your body.

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.450
You want to be as active as possible,

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:18.610
to the safest degree.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:19.443
Why?

00:07:19.443 --> 00:07:21.540
Because you actually
are training your heart.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:22.550
You're burning calories.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:24.040
You're keeping your metabolism up.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:25.750
All these things are really positive

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:27.515
to decrease cardiac event risk,

00:07:27.515 --> 00:07:30.313
especially when you've already
had a first heart attack.

00:07:30.313 --> 00:07:31.890
- [CPR Trainer] ABC. Okay?

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:32.841
And that stands for

00:07:32.841 --> 00:07:33.674
- [CPR Trainer and Dr. Mike] Airway.

00:07:33.674 --> 00:07:34.507
Breathing.

00:07:34.507 --> 00:07:35.340
- [Trainer] And circulation.
- [Dr. Mike] And compressions.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:36.173
- Okay.

00:07:36.173 --> 00:07:37.006
- I threw in the compressions just

00:07:37.006 --> 00:07:38.580
cause I love the compressions.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:39.980
Because if there's no circulation,

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:40.813
what do you do?

00:07:40.813 --> 00:07:41.646
(soft chimes)

00:07:41.646 --> 00:07:43.031
I heard you say it.

00:07:43.031 --> 00:07:45.400
- I can't keep doing this forever.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:46.750
- It's been twenty seconds.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:49.073
- Two minutes.

00:07:49.073 --> 00:07:49.906
- Call it.

00:07:49.906 --> 00:07:50.739
- Call it.

00:07:50.739 --> 00:07:52.990
No, you do it until you realize

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:54.280
that it is...

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:56.043
futile.

00:07:56.043 --> 00:07:56.876
Just to make it clear,

00:07:56.876 --> 00:07:59.000
I feel like this gets
confused for most people

00:07:59.000 --> 00:07:59.970
when they watch these shows.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.390
If someone has a heart attack

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:02.330
it doesn't always mean

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:03.500
that they need chest compressions.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.710
I've literally seen someone
discuss this before.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:06.543
Where they're like,

00:08:06.543 --> 00:08:08.447
"Oh my God, my family member's
having a heart attack.

00:08:08.447 --> 00:08:09.690
"Start chest compressions!"

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:10.540
No, no, no, no, no.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.730
Yes, heart attacks can
sometimes lead to your heart

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:14.660
stopping where you need
chest compressions.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.990
Not all heart attacks
need chest compressions.

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:18.070
Person's awake and alert,

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:19.290
they don't need chest compressions.

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.031
- [CPR Trainer] You're losing him.

00:08:21.031 --> 00:08:22.810
- Oh, those are bad chest compressions.

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:24.290
Not deep enough, too fast.

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:25.123
You want to do it to the tune of

00:08:25.123 --> 00:08:26.700
a hundred beats per minute.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:27.720
There's a lot of famous songs

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:28.640
that you could do it to.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.330
I like "Stayin' Alive".

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:31.220
I would sing it,

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.620
I'm not trying to get demonetized.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:33.690
- [CPR Trainer] Okay. Too fast.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:34.523
Everyone.

00:08:34.523 --> 00:08:35.500
We need to pump at a pace of

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:36.670
100 beats per minute

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:37.820
- Oh, okay.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.100
That's uh, hard to keep track.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:40.051
How many is that per hour?

00:08:40.051 --> 00:08:41.460
- (Dr. Mike laughs) Per hour!

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:42.860
- Check for an organ donor card.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:44.940
If he has one, we only
have minutes to harvest.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:46.070
- He has no wallet. I checked.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:46.960
- He is an organ donor.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:47.793
- He is!

00:08:47.793 --> 00:08:49.330
Give me some ice in a styrofoam bucket.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:50.163
- Whoa!

00:08:50.163 --> 00:08:50.996
- [Dwight] Here we go.

00:08:50.996 --> 00:08:52.361
- Did he just whip out a knife?

00:08:52.361 --> 00:08:54.463
- (hip-hop music) I feel
like a banana split.

00:08:55.765 --> 00:08:56.598
Woo!

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.678
(high pitch scream)

00:08:59.678 --> 00:09:01.480
- Oh, did we tear a groin muscle?

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:03.630
When office parties get buck wild.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:04.463
I also don't know,

00:09:04.463 --> 00:09:06.920
he could have injured his testicular area.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.690
- I was dancing and I did a split

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:10.660
and I landed on my car keys in my pocket.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:13.750
- Oh! I did not expect
that to be the diagnosis.

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:15.500
What is the ICD-10 code for that?

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:16.700
What do you think is the worst story,

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.130
crashed a car because of a tiny spider

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:21.000
or tore scrotum while dancing?

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:21.833
Don't know.

00:09:21.833 --> 00:09:22.970
Click here to watch my reaction

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.320
to the "Friends" medical scenes,

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:25.580
one of my favorite shows.

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:26.890
And don't forget to buy some merch

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:28.220
to help support Americares.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:29.130
That's down below.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.063
As always stay happy and healthy.

00:09:31.938 --> 00:09:34.855
(electronic music)

